WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:03.382
Welcome back to Nagarjuna's lessons.

00:00:04.490 --> 00:00:06.360
I made a mistake at the end of the last lesson.

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:09.730
I said we had finished this chapter,
but we're not actually finished yet.

00:00:09.730 --> 00:00:12.640
We're still in the same chapter, I think it's Chapter 16, about being

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:17.640
bound by samsara

00:00:18.590 --> 00:00:20.257
and attaining

00:00:20.257 --> 00:00:24.480
nirvana, that is, liberation from samsara. The

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:27.350
latter part of this chapter

00:00:27.350 --> 00:00:29.900
doesn't need to be taught for a long time,

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:34.860
so I think you might want to take a look at what

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:38.460
we can be grateful for.

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:41.420
Look at the work of those who tried so hard to translate these texts

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:44.620
and to find them.

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:49.620
You're a bit like at the bottom of the food chain right now,

00:00:50.130 --> 00:00:52.497
talking about the chain of knowledge.

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:57.810
You have all this information, all these

00:00:57.810 --> 00:01:02.120
teachings,

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:07.120
because of the hard work of others who searched for books, cataloged them,

00:01:07.490 --> 00:01:10.790
scanned, typed, and preserved these classic books.

00:01:10.790 --> 00:01:14.170
I'm talking about the Asian Classics Heritage Library

00:01:14.170 --> 00:01:16.175
and all their efforts.

00:01:16.175 --> 00:01:20.730
I did the easy part;

00:01:20.730 --> 00:01:24.480
I just explained things.

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:28.550
I was able to get these books
because of other people who have

00:01:28.550 --> 00:01:32.840
worked so hard for over thirty years to find and borrow these books

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:34.980
and then brought them to me.

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:37.860
These people travel all over the world,

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:39.490
searching for books and bringing them home

00:01:39.490 --> 00:01:42.700
so I can sit down, read, and share them with you.

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:47.070
So I want to thank John Brady and the
entire team at the Asian Classics Heritage Library.  To

00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:51.513
express my gratitude for your hard work,

00:01:51.513 --> 00:01:53.386
and to the entire Gold Club

00:01:53.386 --> 00:01:56.550
and the "clubs" around the world,

00:01:56.550 --> 00:02:01.230
all of you have worked so hard to raise funds.

00:02:01.230 --> 00:02:04.393
Sometimes I think, if I were you,

00:02:04.393 --> 00:02:06.520
I might think: how will they use this money?

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:08.700
Where will the money go?

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:10.670
I think you will be very happy

00:02:10.670 --> 00:02:15.670
to see that your donations have ultimately made these courses possible.

00:02:16.157 --> 00:02:18.965
First of all, the

00:02:18.965 --> 00:02:23.965
donations were made 20 to 22 years ago.

00:02:24.670 --> 00:02:28.390
They went to...

00:02:28.390 --> 00:02:33.390
We tried to find Master Chuni's books. In

00:02:33.730 --> 00:02:38.220
those days, it was very

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:40.640
difficult to find any of Master Chuni's books.

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:44.850
My teacher showed me a book by Master Chuni at the time, and

00:02:44.850 --> 00:02:46.830
I thought to myself, wow!

00:02:46.830 --> 00:02:49.860
I studied it, and he had 250 such books.

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:51.213
I said, "Rinpoche, please give them all to me."

00:02:51.213 --> 00:02:53.180
He said, "It's no use, you can't have them."

00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:54.710
I said, "What do you mean by 'can't have them'?"

00:02:54.710 --> 00:02:57.637
He said, "We made a mistake before."

00:02:57.637 --> 00:02:58.917
I asked, "Who are you referring to?"

00:02:58.917 --> 00:03:01.940
He said, "My monastery, Sera Me Monastery, we made a mistake."

00:03:01.940 --> 00:03:03.450
I asked, "What mistake?"

00:03:03.450 --> 00:03:06.440
He said, "We didn't want other monasteries to

00:03:06.440 --> 00:03:09.630
get Master Chögyal's books

00:03:09.630 --> 00:03:13.200
because that way, we could win all the monasteries' debating competitions. So

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:16.630
we carefully preserved all of Master Chögyal's books."

00:03:16.630 --> 00:03:17.900
I asked, "And what happened afterward?"

00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:20.290
He said, "We put them in the library."

00:03:20.290 --> 00:03:22.050
I asked, "And what happened to the library afterward?"

00:03:22.050 --> 00:03:23.730
He said, "The library was bombed during the revolution,

00:03:23.730 --> 00:03:26.600
and then everything was burned down."

00:03:26.600 --> 00:03:28.560
Every book was burned.

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:31.920
We made a foolish mistake
by putting all the books in one place.

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:35.020
Perhaps this is also a manifestation of karma, right?

00:03:35.020 --> 00:03:35.880
Nobody could get them.

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:37.680
They were all burned.

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:39.870
Then we left.

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:41.580
That's why I'm here teaching you now.

00:03:41.580 --> 00:03:46.580
Then we started looking for

00:03:46.750 --> 00:03:49.410
any books about Master Chöni.

00:03:49.410 --> 00:03:53.810
Finally, Jason Dunbar, an
early employee of the Asian Classical Heritage Library,

00:03:53.810 --> 00:03:58.590
went to Chöni temples in western China and

00:03:58.590 --> 00:04:02.280
asked to scan Master Chöni's books. He

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:07.280
got those books.

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:10.933
He got all the books.
I want to show you one of them. Back then,

00:04:12.010 --> 00:04:15.680


00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:20.613
in the Chöni area of ​​western China, the

00:04:21.500 --> 00:04:22.475
printing presses weren't very good,

00:04:22.475 --> 00:04:24.870
but you can see this is a cool engraving.

00:04:24.870 --> 00:04:28.130
This is what it looks like.

00:04:28.130 --> 00:04:33.130
The letters on the left side—

00:04:33.890 --> 00:04:36.400
they say RTZA…—are
written in some Western language. It

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:40.307
means RTZA BA SHES RAB KYI TI KA.

00:04:40.307 --> 00:04:42.782
RTZA BA SHES RAB KYI TI KA TI

00:04:42.782 --> 00:04:44.373
KA means commentary

00:04:44.373 --> 00:04:46.940
and RTZA BA SHES RAB, the root text of Prajna.

00:04:46.940 --> 00:04:51.320
You'll find the book's name at the very bottom,

00:04:51.320 --> 00:04:54.280
and then the page number is

00:04:54.280 --> 00:04:56.940
@... BDUN, sorry

00:04:56.940 --> 00:05:00.250
@... TRUS DUN, it's page 67.

00:05:00.250 --> 00:05:04.637
Then there will be a volume number, probably LNGA.

00:05:06.703 --> 00:05:09.520
This means the collection contains many scrolls. It

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:12.770
's common for pages to separate from the book

00:05:12.770 --> 00:05:16.310
because they aren't bound together.

00:05:16.310 --> 00:05:18.990
You can put the book back after finding it

00:05:18.990 --> 00:05:21.260
because teachers often give students a few pages to

00:05:21.260 --> 00:05:23.530
memorize and then return them the next day,

00:05:23.530 --> 00:05:25.442
but the students never do.

00:05:25.442 --> 00:05:30.442
This book comes from the Chuni Temple, and

00:05:30.830 --> 00:05:33.780
it's the only one we could find.

00:05:33.780 --> 00:05:38.180
Therefore, whenever we have doubts, we

00:05:38.180 --> 00:05:40.593
can't verify it.

00:05:41.790 --> 00:05:44.220
There are no comparable versions

00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:47.190
because there are no other engravings available for reference.

00:05:47.190 --> 00:05:49.530
Sometimes we encounter problems with the engravings,

00:05:49.530 --> 00:05:52.970
such as being unsure if the inscription above is correct.

00:05:52.970 --> 00:05:56.430
Finally, we went to Russia and

00:05:56.430 --> 00:06:01.430
found approximately 141,000 manuscripts in Western languages.

00:06:03.010 --> 00:06:04.607
In the King's collection, the

00:06:04.607 --> 00:06:08.970
Russian Tsar amassed a vast collection.

00:06:08.970 --> 00:06:10.230
He was a bibliophile.

00:06:10.230 --> 00:06:14.507
Russians are deeply ingrained bibliophiles; they love books.

00:06:14.507 --> 00:06:17.380
He collected 141,000 books.

00:06:17.380 --> 00:06:19.410
We found a second one, which

00:06:19.410 --> 00:06:22.083
you can see in the next picture.

00:06:23.110 --> 00:06:26.310
This is a work by Master Chuni.

00:06:26.310 --> 00:06:30.790
Better engraving quality; you can see it's clearer.

00:06:31.940 --> 00:06:35.682
We spent 12 years cataloging those books.

00:06:38.174 --> 00:06:41.640
12 years, and not just photocopying or scanning.

00:06:41.640 --> 00:06:45.920
12 years!  Years ago, we just copied down names.

00:06:45.920 --> 00:06:49.350
We had three full-time employees.

00:06:49.350 --> 00:06:52.720
Once, we saw a

00:06:52.720 --> 00:06:56.416
contract that said

00:06:56.416 --> 00:07:00.500
we could photocopy books from the monastery.

00:07:00.500 --> 00:07:02.620
We got permission to photocopy.

00:07:02.620 --> 00:07:05.320
I looked at the printed copy again.

00:07:05.320 --> 00:07:09.400
If,

00:07:09.400 --> 00:07:12.460
when the rollers were printing ink on the wooden blocks,

00:07:12.460 --> 00:07:17.270
the person holding the paper probably wasn't looking at the girl,
but at something else, then

00:07:17.270 --> 00:07:22.020
these large stripes would be printed on the paper.

00:07:22.020 --> 00:07:24.810
You can see that black patch on the right.

00:07:24.810 --> 00:07:27.320
Geshe-la had to guess

00:07:27.320 --> 00:07:30.160
what was actually carved on that wooden block.

00:07:30.160 --> 00:07:33.690
But sometimes it's really hard to guess.

00:07:33.690 --> 00:07:38.690
So having multiple wooden blocks is important. It's important

00:07:38.850 --> 00:07:41.830
to go around the world to find other versions.

00:07:41.830 --> 00:07:45.030


00:07:45.030 --> 00:07:49.080
Flying from eastern China to Russia requires funding. Getting

00:07:49.080 --> 00:07:51.630
permission from the Russians to scan and input their books for

00:07:51.630 --> 00:07:53.730
12 years requires funding.

00:07:53.730 --> 00:07:56.080
This is where your donations will go.

00:07:56.080 --> 00:07:58.010
It requires a lot of money.

00:07:58.010 --> 00:08:01.540
We've already spent $5-10 million,

00:08:01.540 --> 00:08:04.110
and we've only completed about five percent.

00:08:04.110 --> 00:08:08.900
If we want to learn these courses,

00:08:08.900 --> 00:08:11.380
want to break free from the chains of samsara, and

00:08:13.080 --> 00:08:15.983
rescue those bound by samsara,

00:08:17.410 --> 00:08:20.270
we need these books and money.

00:08:20.270 --> 00:08:24.340
Flying to ancient libraries everywhere also requires money.

00:08:24.340 --> 00:08:26.310
And over the years, for

00:08:26.310 --> 00:08:29.100
12 years, all of this requires money.

00:08:29.100 --> 00:08:30.810
Those who live in St. Petersburg, the

00:08:30.810 --> 00:08:34.393
most expensive city in Europe,

00:08:35.240 --> 00:08:37.140
because you can't grow anything there.

00:08:37.140 --> 00:08:39.510
When I went there,

00:08:39.510 --> 00:08:40.735
they said not to spend too much money on dinner.

00:08:40.735 --> 00:08:42.980
I said okay.

00:08:42.980 --> 00:08:46.930
Then I went to dinner.
I said I just wanted a salad.

00:08:46.930 --> 00:08:49.120
They gave me a salad.

00:08:49.120 --> 00:08:50.950
The next day I said I didn't spend too much money on dinner.

00:08:50.950 --> 00:08:51.783
What did you eat?

00:08:51.783 --> 00:08:52.760
I ordered a salad. Oh my

00:08:52.760 --> 00:08:54.930
god, this is the most expensive thing.

00:08:54.930 --> 00:08:58.000
It costs almost $50.

00:08:58.000 --> 00:08:59.230
I asked why?

00:08:59.230 --> 00:09:01.740
They said: "You're in Russia, it's too cold there, you

00:09:01.740 --> 00:09:04.100
can't grow vegetables here, so..."

00:09:04.100 --> 00:09:06.447
You should order some lamb or something,

00:09:08.230 --> 00:09:11.450
so staying there costs money.

00:09:11.450 --> 00:09:13.870
Staying there for 12 years costs a lot of money,

00:09:13.870 --> 00:09:16.390
and there are all sorts of problems.

00:09:16.390 --> 00:09:18.460
One of our employees was kidnapped by the Russian mafia.

00:09:20.530 --> 00:09:23.490
They said they'd kill him for $50,000 or they'd kill him.

00:09:23.490 --> 00:09:25.753
You know that's expensive.

00:09:26.930 --> 00:09:29.900
Let me show you something else, which is

00:09:29.900 --> 00:09:32.060
why John Brady is so great

00:09:32.060 --> 00:09:34.780
and why his projects are so worth your help.

00:09:34.780 --> 00:09:37.090
Let's look at the next slide.

00:09:38.480 --> 00:09:43.280
This is a woodblock print from the Quni Temple,

00:09:43.280 --> 00:09:46.160
printed around 1700 AD. That

00:09:46.160 --> 00:09:49.280
means it was carved around 1700 AD.

00:09:49.280 --> 00:09:52.646
If you understand Western languages,

00:09:55.105 --> 00:09:57.080
please look at the fourth line.

00:09:57.080 --> 00:09:59.632
Tim, can you read this slowly?  Let's start

00:09:59.632 --> 00:10:01.505
reading from GZHAN

00:10:03.040 --> 00:10:04.960
!

00:10:04.960 --> 00:10:09.960
RGYU DANG ‘BRAS BU

00:10:11.568 --> 00:10:13.460
NYID DANG

00:10:13.460 --> 00:10:14.745
Continue

00:10:14.745 --> 00:10:16.353
DANG NI

00:10:17.318 --> 00:10:21.051
MTSAN GZHI

00:10:22.251 --> 00:10:24.064
NYID DANG

00:10:24.064 --> 00:10:25.843
Very good It

00:10:25.843 --> 00:10:30.669
says ‘BRAS BU NYID DANG NI MTSAN GZHI NYID

00:10:30.669 --> 00:10:31.910
Geshe teacher is translating

00:10:31.910 --> 00:10:33.310
Geshe teacher is preparing this lesson

00:10:33.310 --> 00:10:37.550
Geshe teacher is almost going crazy because the words on this seem illogical There are

00:10:37.550 --> 00:10:39.540
some problems here

00:10:39.540 --> 00:10:43.230
Many, many years later

00:10:43.230 --> 00:10:45.620
you can feel that something is missing.

00:10:45.620 --> 00:10:47.500
For example, this piece of paper is missing something here By

00:10:47.500 --> 00:10:50.410
the way, Tim, you can appreciate

00:10:50.410 --> 00:10:54.750
Here is a pair of words
Here are several pairs of words

00:10:54.750 --> 00:10:58.960
Here are RGYU and ‘BRAS BU, cause and effect

00:10:58.960 --> 00:11:02.360
Next below are TSOR BA and TSOR BA PO

00:11:02.360 --> 00:11:05.170
feeling and feeling

00:11:05.170 --> 00:11:07.870
Then in these words there

00:11:07.870 --> 00:11:10.880
are “undefined things”

00:11:10.880 --> 00:11:15.220
and “defined things”
These are some pairs of words

00:11:15.220 --> 00:11:18.930
But there is only one single word in the middle

00:11:18.930 --> 00:11:21.520
Logically there should be a word missing The

00:11:21.520 --> 00:11:26.190
meaning expressed here is “defined”, what word is needed

00:11:26.190 --> 00:11:28.293
“definition” MTSAN NYID

00:11:28.293 --> 00:11:30.400
Missing MTSAN NYID

00:11:30.400 --> 00:11:32.850
So Geshe spent three hours

00:11:32.850 --> 00:11:34.890
struggling and struggling,

00:11:34.890 --> 00:11:36.400
until he discovered that the problem was with the woodblock. The

00:11:36.400 --> 00:11:37.910
original carving of the woodblock was flawed; the

00:11:37.910 --> 00:11:40.910
person who printed it was probably distracted and looking at something else, so they

00:11:40.910 --> 00:11:44.323
didn't carve the word "definition" on the woodblock.

00:11:47.220 --> 00:11:49.703
So what did Geshe do?

00:11:50.776 --> 00:11:52.410
Yes, I'm going to find another woodblock to compare it to.

00:11:52.410 --> 00:11:54.550
But if the Asian Classics Heritage Library

00:11:54.550 --> 00:11:57.260
didn't have the money to fly to St. Petersburg and stay there for 12 years,

00:11:57.260 --> 00:11:59.710
I wouldn't have a second woodblock, understand?

00:11:59.710 --> 00:12:01.600
This is why we need the "Golden Club."

00:12:01.600 --> 00:12:03.900
This is why you should support the Golden Club,

00:12:03.900 --> 00:12:08.900
even if it's just $5 or $1. Many hands make

00:12:09.080 --> 00:12:12.970
light work.

00:12:12.970 --> 00:12:15.610
This is the original intention of the Golden Club.

00:12:15.610 --> 00:12:18.900
We are not very rich people

00:12:18.900 --> 00:12:23.590
, but a group of people who each insist on donating $1, $5, or $10.

00:12:23.590 --> 00:12:25.500
Then, by pooling our strength, we lifted the Titanic.

00:12:25.500 --> 00:12:27.990
So I want to participate with you.

00:12:27.990 --> 00:12:31.590
Geshe knew that something was missing from this woodblock.

00:12:31.590 --> 00:12:34.140
So Geshe went to find a second woodblock and a
book to compare. Let's take a look.

00:12:35.610 --> 00:12:38.440
By the way, I made this in the car this morning. Tim

00:12:38.440 --> 00:12:42.283
, please read the third line:

00:12:44.889 --> 00:12:48.806
MTSAN NYID DANG MTSAN GZHI NYID.

00:12:50.050 --> 00:12:51.823
What does this mean?

00:12:53.060 --> 00:12:56.713
The word that Geshe predicted should be here. It's

00:12:58.550 --> 00:13:01.400
about "defining" MTSAN NYID and MTSAN GZHI.

00:13:01.400 --> 00:13:04.215
Go back to the previous page, go back to the previous page, zoom in a bit.

00:13:06.610 --> 00:13:08.993
Now, a $100 reward.

00:13:11.384 --> 00:13:15.230
How exactly did they correct the woodblock?

00:13:15.230 --> 00:13:17.900
Someone corrected it 200 years ago.

00:13:17.900 --> 00:13:21.180
Someone like Geshe sat there for three hours

00:13:21.180 --> 00:13:24.860
and said there was a problem, and then corrected it.

00:13:24.860 --> 00:13:27.777
But how do you correct a wooden block?

00:13:28.740 --> 00:13:33.740
You have to scrape off the original letters, carve out a square,

00:13:35.740 --> 00:13:39.310
then take another piece of wood, carve it to the same size,

00:13:39.310 --> 00:13:43.020
glue it in, and then recarve it.

00:13:43.020 --> 00:13:45.170
But there's no place to recarve it

00:13:45.170 --> 00:13:47.880
because you need to add a word. What do you do then?

00:13:47.880 --> 00:13:50.010
Then you'll have to shrink the font!

00:13:50.010 --> 00:13:53.180
Look at the third line, it's cool.

00:13:53.180 --> 00:13:54.700
You shrink everything.

00:13:54.700 --> 00:13:58.154
This person is working hard to carve words.

00:13:58.154 --> 00:14:00.978
Hey, can you add two more words?

00:14:00.978 --> 00:14:02.570
What?

00:14:02.570 --> 00:14:03.860
Come on, you can do it!

00:14:03.860 --> 00:14:06.000
You can see this guy sitting there carving.

00:14:06.000 --> 00:14:08.090
Light engraving takes about

00:14:08.090 --> 00:14:10.440
three to four hours.

00:14:10.440 --> 00:14:12.690
He's trying to make this line even smaller.

00:14:12.690 --> 00:14:16.830
When you look closely at this picture, you can see... It's really

00:14:16.830 --> 00:14:20.820
cool to me.

00:14:20.820 --> 00:14:25.410
Someone cut off the original missing piece of wood and glued on a newly carved piece of wood.

00:14:25.410 --> 00:14:29.340
That's why it looks smaller, you get it?  Is

00:14:29.340 --> 00:14:30.510
n't that cool?

00:14:30.510 --> 00:14:33.580
If the Golden Club doesn't send someone

00:14:33.580 --> 00:14:35.773
to the St. Petersburg Library,

00:14:37.290 --> 00:14:39.120
then I can't explain this book to you

00:14:39.120 --> 00:14:41.900
because there are some problems with it, understand?

00:14:41.900 --> 00:14:44.040
Then it'll just keep going wrong forever.

00:14:44.040 --> 00:14:46.650
If we don't correct it,

00:14:46.650 --> 00:14:50.009
believe me, nobody will care. [laughs]

00:14:50.009 --> 00:14:50.842
Okay?

00:14:50.842 --> 00:14:52.010
So I think that's cool. Okay

00:14:52.010 --> 00:14:54.820
, now let's look at that impossible book.

00:14:54.820 --> 00:14:58.180
This book is already impossible, but

00:14:58.180 --> 00:15:01.859
it gets even harder if something is missing.

00:15:01.859 --> 00:15:03.457
Okay, let's begin. ,

00:15:03.457 --> 00:15:04.840
GAL TE NYE BAR LEN ‘CHING NA,

00:15:04.840 --> 00:15:06.448
,NYE BAR LEN BCAS ‘CHING MI ‘GYUR,

00:15:06.448 --> 00:15:09.206
,NYE BAR LEN MED MI ‘CHING STE, ,

00:15:09.206 --> 00:15:11.322
GNAS SKABS GANG ZHIG ‘CHING BAR ‘GYUR,

00:15:11.322 --> 00:15:13.870
Don't forget we're talking about being bound by chains.

00:15:13.870 --> 00:15:15.880
‘CHING BA’ here means “bound”,

00:15:15.880 --> 00:15:19.463
chained. Who is bound by what?

00:15:20.720 --> 00:15:22.609
What binds us?

00:15:22.609 --> 00:15:23.540
[Student's reply]

00:15:23.540 --> 00:15:26.863
Consciousness and body, we have misunderstood them.

00:15:28.089 --> 00:15:30.810
We are locked in an imperfect body and consciousness.

00:15:30.810 --> 00:15:33.340
We are locked in a physical body.

00:15:33.340 --> 00:15:36.590
We are locked in an impure consciousness.

00:15:36.590 --> 00:15:39.573
[Laughs] This is the bondage, understand?

00:15:40.460 --> 00:15:42.301
Who is bound?

00:15:43.580 --> 00:15:45.300
Mike is bound.

00:15:45.300 --> 00:15:49.830
Mike is bound in a terrible body and mind.

00:15:50.700 --> 00:15:52.450
Is Mike the chain?

00:15:53.400 --> 00:15:55.020
I mean, when you lock someone up,

00:15:55.020 --> 00:15:57.550
lock them to the wall in a dungeon,

00:15:57.550 --> 00:16:00.460
you chain a prisoner to the wall, and the

00:16:00.460 --> 00:16:02.480
chain is part of the wall?

00:16:02.480 --> 00:16:05.563
So the chain is part of the prisoner?

00:16:07.084 --> 00:16:08.620
No, it's somewhere

00:16:08.620 --> 00:16:11.280
between the prisoner and the wall, right?

00:16:11.280 --> 00:16:12.740
Understand?
 
318
00:16:12,740 --&gt; 00:16:15,690
So, by the way,

00:16:15.690 --> 00:16:18.560
in Abhidharma, the chain is a separate entity.

00:16:18.560 --> 00:16:19.840
This is very interesting. The

00:16:19.840 --> 00:16:24.480
book says there is a body and consciousness, and here is a Pachi. The

00:16:24.480 --> 00:16:27.630
chain is some mysterious third party that

00:16:27.630 --> 00:16:32.421
locks the Pachi in his body and consciousness.

00:16:32.421 --> 00:16:35.330
Nagarjuna said that

00:16:35.330 --> 00:16:39.110
the chain is either part of the body and consciousness or

00:16:39.110 --> 00:16:40.470
part of the person.

00:16:40.470 --> 00:16:42.370
Who is locked in the body and consciousness?

00:16:42.370 --> 00:16:45.210
But where does the chain go?

00:16:45.210 --> 00:16:47.677
What happens when a person dies? The chain falls off. Are

00:16:47.677 --> 00:16:50.592
chains all that's left in the entire universe?

00:16:51.930 --> 00:16:54.340
Who is bound?

00:16:54.340 --> 00:16:55.510
Who is bound?

00:16:55.510 --> 00:16:58.971
I skipped right to the last line:

00:16:58.971 --> 00:17:01.887
SONG DANG MA SONG BGOM PAS BSTAN

00:17:01.887 --> 00:17:04.781
SONG DANG MA SONG BGOM PAS BSTAN

00:17:04.781 --> 00:17:09.680
$100, what does BGOM mean, $100.

00:17:10.930 --> 00:17:13.391
He's right, this isn't meditation.

00:17:13.391 --> 00:17:14.370
[Students answer]

00:17:14.370 --> 00:17:17.550
No, this has nothing to do with meditation.

00:17:17.550 --> 00:17:19.589
We've learned it a hundred times.

00:17:19.589 --> 00:17:22.880
[Students respond] The

00:17:22.880 --> 00:17:26.199
spelling is very similar, and the pronunciation is the same.

00:17:26.199 --> 00:17:27.349
Because it has a prefix.

00:17:31.390 --> 00:17:34.970
$50, I'll give you a hint.
[Students laugh]

00:17:34.970 --> 00:17:37.640
It's appeared before. It

00:17:40.360 --> 00:17:43.277
appeared in Chapter 2, $25.

00:17:47.090 --> 00:17:50.620
Walking, $25 for Gails. Amazing.

00:17:51.960 --> 00:17:53.279


00:17:53.279 --> 00:17:54.660
You cheated. It's

00:17:54.660 --> 00:17:57.162
okay, don't tell me. Yes,

00:17:57.162 --> 00:17:58.360
walking.

00:17:58.360 --> 00:18:00.727
What does this have to do with chains?

00:18:00.727 --> 00:18:01.860
$100.

00:18:01.860 --> 00:18:03.350
[Students answer]

00:18:03.350 --> 00:18:05.790
Yes, he's showing me. Yes,

00:18:05.790 --> 00:18:06.960
here. Where

00:18:06.960 --> 00:18:09.340
…

00:18:09.340 --> 00:18:11.650
[Students answer] Where is

00:18:11.650 --> 00:18:14.650
that part? A part of the

00:18:14.650 --> 00:18:18.990
road you've already walked. There

00:18:18.990 --> 00:18:21.130
's a part you haven't walked yet.

00:18:21.130 --> 00:18:24.450
And there's a part you're walking.

00:18:24.450 --> 00:18:26.410
The place you're walking should be somewhere in between,

00:18:26.410 --> 00:18:27.670
but you can't define it.

00:18:27.670 --> 00:18:32.670
This is very similar to the idea of ​​chains. The

00:18:33.010 --> 00:18:37.160
middle point, the part you're walking on the road.

00:18:37.160 --> 00:18:39.328
BGOM is

00:18:39.328 --> 00:18:41.270
always in an active state,

00:18:41.270 --> 00:18:42.730
stepping on something, but not actually walking. It's about where its foot

00:18:42.730 --> 00:18:46.060
lands

00:18:46.060 --> 00:18:47.660
on the road. So where is the

00:18:47.660 --> 00:18:50.805
current part of the road?

00:18:50.805 --> 00:18:53.040
[Sound]

00:18:53.040 --> 00:18:54.720
Where is it right now? That is, the

00:18:54.720 --> 00:18:57.095
position of the chain. Do you

00:18:59.863 --> 00:19:01.820
understand?  That's cool, right?

00:19:01.820 --> 00:19:03.104
Where are the chains?

00:19:03.104 --> 00:19:07.797
Like between you and your body and consciousness

00:19:07.797 --> 00:19:09.177
[laughs].

00:19:09.177 --> 00:19:12.063
So you understand what Nagarjuna meant by "

00:19:12.063 --> 00:19:15.360
between you and your body and consciousness."

00:19:15.360 --> 00:19:17.663
Let's go back to these verses:

00:19:18.981 --> 00:19:20.744
GAL TE NYE BAR LEN ‘CHING NA

00:19:20.744 --> 00:19:25.744
If you are bound in your body and consciousness,

00:19:27.126 --> 00:19:29.420
NYE BAR LEN BCAS ‘CHING MI ‘GYUR,

00:19:29.420 --> 00:19:31.777
you are not bound.

00:19:36.490 --> 00:19:38.013
If the chains are part of your body and consciousness,

00:19:38.013 --> 00:19:40.420
then the chains are not part of you.

00:19:40.420 --> 00:19:41.875
How about that?

00:19:41.875 --> 00:19:45.780
NYE BAR LEN MED MI ‘CHING STE

00:19:45.780 --> 00:19:49.570
Then, if you release your body and consciousness, you will

00:19:49.570 --> 00:19:51.687
no longer be bound by chains.

00:19:52.530 --> 00:19:54.070
If you die, you will no longer be bound by chains, and

00:19:54.070 --> 00:19:56.300
you should attain Nirvana.

00:19:56.300 --> 00:19:59.320
If the chains are part of your defiled body and consciousness,

00:19:59.320 --> 00:20:03.030
then as you continuously let go of your body and consciousness,

00:20:03.030 --> 00:20:05.805
you should be able to break free of the shackles for a period of time.

00:20:06.814 --> 00:20:08.650
GNAS SKABS GANG ZHIG ‘CHING BAR ‘GYUR

00:20:08.650 --> 00:20:11.323
So where is the point at which you are bound?

00:20:13.130 --> 00:20:16.150
Same argument, you tell me where it comes from.

00:20:16.150 --> 00:20:20.193
$100, wow, you guys made a lot of money today.

00:20:21.810 --> 00:20:22.930
Ready?

00:20:22.930 --> 00:20:24.639
Everyone does this… (starts to wake up)

00:20:27.420 --> 00:20:30.333
Before that person was bound, did the chains exist?

00:20:34.320 --> 00:20:37.130
Geshe-la, what's the $100 reward for?

00:20:37.130 --> 00:20:39.090
If you can recite a verse from before this course series, you

00:20:39.090 --> 00:20:40.783
get $100.

00:20:41.815 --> 00:20:43.615
[Student response]

00:20:43.615 --> 00:20:47.260
That's good, but it has to be before that verse. By the

00:20:47.260 --> 00:20:50.690
way, the title of Chapter Nine is

00:20:50.690 --> 00:20:53.230
"Was There Before?" The

00:20:53.230 --> 00:20:57.990
title of Chapter Nine is "

00:20:57.990 --> 00:21:00.023
Exploring Whether Something Existed Before" (The Chapter on Observing the Abode).

00:21:01.070 --> 00:21:04.078
It talks about one of the senses (the sense organs).

00:21:04.078 --> 00:21:05.128
[Student response]

00:21:05.128 --> 00:21:07.870
Yes, when you look at something... is the

00:21:07.870 --> 00:21:10.300
eye already there?

00:21:10.300 --> 00:21:13.253
Is that little girl with the telescope here?

00:21:14.470 --> 00:21:19.470
She went outside and saw the stars there?

00:21:19.790 --> 00:21:22.310
Which came first, the perceiver or the "seeing"?

00:21:22.310 --> 00:21:24.310
Which one came first: the

00:21:24.310 --> 00:21:26.710
little girl who went outside with the telescope

00:21:26.710 --> 00:21:29.830
and looked at the stars?  How does it work?

00:21:29.830 --> 00:21:32.860
Was it the little girl with the telescope who went out of the house first to look at

00:21:32.860 --> 00:21:35.983
the stars?

00:21:36.995 --> 00:21:39.430
[Student Response]

00:21:39.430 --> 00:21:42.580
She had to do it because there had to be a telescope first,

00:21:42.580 --> 00:21:44.633
as astronomy says.

00:21:45.700 --> 00:21:50.430
But she couldn't because she wasn't an astronomer

00:21:50.430 --> 00:21:52.180
until she looked at the stars.

00:21:52.180 --> 00:21:53.790
So the whole thing was impossible.

00:21:53.790 --> 00:21:56.100
Now, bringing it to this:

00:21:56.100 --> 00:21:58.140
chains, which came first, the chains

00:21:58.140 --> 00:21:59.566
or the bound?

00:21:59.566 --> 00:22:02.000
[Student vaguely answers:
You can't be considered a bound person until you're chained.]

00:22:02.000 --> 00:22:03.230
Yes, you can't be a bound person

00:22:03.230 --> 00:22:05.130
until you're chained.

00:22:05.130 --> 00:22:08.160
But before you can be chained, there must be chains. The

00:22:08.160 --> 00:22:11.833
whole thing is a bit confusing.

00:22:13.457 --> 00:22:17.115
The point here is,

00:22:17.115 --> 00:22:18.815
if there's a problem in the kitchen,

00:22:18.815 --> 00:22:21.320
you have to figure out why your husband is unhappy.

00:22:21.320 --> 00:22:23.960
You have to look for other options.

00:22:23.960 --> 00:22:25.850
Maybe it all comes from you.

00:22:25.850 --> 00:22:28.025
Maybe it all comes from you.

00:22:28.025 --> 00:22:29.730
That's the key.

00:22:29.730 --> 00:22:31.590
Maybe it all comes from you.

00:22:31.590 --> 00:22:34.440
Maybe you created the telescope, and then you created the girl.

00:22:34.440 --> 00:22:37.440
You created astronomy.

00:22:37.440 --> 00:22:38.730
Everything comes from you,

00:22:38.730 --> 00:22:41.948
but they are not the cause of each other.

00:22:41.948 --> 00:22:43.400
[Speaks]

00:22:43.400 --> 00:22:47.548
It seems like that,
but it's actually like this…

00:22:47.548 --> 00:22:48.800
understand?

00:22:48.800 --> 00:22:50.396
That's all.

00:22:50.396 --> 00:22:52.210
If you realize this,

00:22:52.210 --> 00:22:54.160
then you can chant the Medicine Buddha mantra.

00:22:56.330 --> 00:22:59.949
But if you think it's (that's how it comes about),
then you're in trouble.

00:22:59.949 --> 00:23:02.240
If you chant the Medicine Buddha mantra (with that mindset),

00:23:02.240 --> 00:23:03.420
you're in trouble.

00:23:03.420 --> 00:23:05.690
If you realize the mantra comes from you, the

00:23:05.690 --> 00:23:08.850
illness comes from you, and the
healing also comes from you,

00:23:08.850 --> 00:23:12.160
then you can chant the mantra, cool, right?

00:23:12.160 --> 00:23:14.810
Next sentence. By the

00:23:14.810 --> 00:23:18.143
